---
import Article from "../../layouts/Article.astro";
import { articles } from "../../data/insights.js";
const article = articles.find((a) => a.slug === "iso-tax-when-your-company-is-acquired");
---
<Article article={article}>

<p>
  An acquisition is usually the first time an employee with incentive stock options has to
  think about them seriously. It is also, in most deals, the last moment at which anything
  can be done. The merger agreement decides the tax treatment, and by the time it reaches
  employees it is signed.
</p>
<p>
  What follows is what actually happens to ISOs in the three common outcomes, and the
  provisions worth reading in your own documents now rather than at closing.
</p>

<h2>Three things can happen to your options</h2>

<h3>1. Cashed out</h3>
<p>
  The most common outcome in a cash deal. Your vested options are cancelled and you receive
  the spread — deal price per share less your exercise price — in cash, usually through
  payroll.
</p>
<p>
  This is a disqualifying disposition. You never held the shares, so there is no capital
  gain treatment available and no holding period to satisfy. The entire spread is ordinary
  compensation income, reported on your W-2, subject to income tax withholding and payroll
  taxes.
</p>
<p>
  The practical consequence is that the favorable ISO treatment you may have been planning
  around simply does not happen. The option is taxed like a cash bonus. For a large grant
  this can push a single year's income into a much higher bracket, trigger the additional
  Medicare tax, and phase out deductions and credits that were available the year before.
</p>

<h3>2. Assumed or rolled over</h3>
<p>
  In a stock deal, the acquirer may assume your options and convert them into options on
  its own shares, adjusting the count and strike price to preserve the economics.
</p>
<p>
  Done correctly, this is not a taxable event. Your original grant date and, if you already
  exercised, your holding period generally carry forward. But the substitution has to meet
  specific requirements to preserve ISO status. If the adjustment gives you a benefit you
  did not have before — a lower ratio of exercise price to value — the options can convert
  to non-qualified options. You will not be told this happened. It shows up later, when a
  broker reports the exercise differently than you expected.
</p>

<h3>3. Cancelled for nothing</h3>
<p>
  If the deal price is at or below your exercise price, underwater options are typically
  cancelled without payment. There is no tax event and no deduction. If you exercised those
  options in a prior year and paid alternative minimum tax on the spread, you may be
  carrying an AMT credit that is now worth more attention than the options ever were.
</p>

<h2>The acceleration trap</h2>

<p>
  This is the provision that surprises people, and it is worth understanding before you
  read your own grant.
</p>
<p>
  ISOs carry a statutory limit: no more than $100,000 of options, measured by the value of
  the underlying stock at grant, may become <em>first exercisable</em> by any one employee in
  any calendar year. Options above that threshold are treated as non-qualified options.
</p>
<p>
  Ordinary vesting schedules are usually designed to stay under the limit. Acceleration
  breaks that design. When a change of control accelerates several years of vesting into a
  single year, a grant that complied comfortably can blow through $100,000 in one moment.
  The excess becomes NSOs by operation of law — no election, no notice, no choice.
</p>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>Why this matters even in a cash deal</strong>
  <p>
    If everything is cashed out anyway, the treatment is ordinary income either way, so the
    conversion looks harmless. It is not harmless if the deal falls through, if you leave
    before closing, or if only part of the position is cashed out. In those cases you are
    left holding options you believe are ISOs and that are not.
  </p>
</div>

<h2>The ninety-day rule</h2>

<p>
  To keep ISO status, options must generally be exercised within three months of ending
  employment. Miss it and they become NSOs — still exercisable if the plan allows, but taxed
  as ordinary income on the spread at exercise.
</p>
<p>
  Acquisitions produce terminations. Redundancies, role eliminations, people who decide not
  to stay. If you leave with vested, unexercised, in-the-money ISOs, the clock starts on your
  last day and it does not care that you were busy.
</p>

<h2>Escrow, holdbacks, and earnouts</h2>

<p>
  Deal proceeds are rarely paid all at once. A portion typically sits in escrow for twelve to
  eighteen months against indemnity claims, and earnouts can run for years.
</p>
<p>
  For option holders cashed out through payroll, the timing question is when the deferred
  amounts become taxable and how they are reported. Amounts held back are usually taxed when
  received, not at closing — which spreads income across years and can be an advantage. But
  it also means a withholding calculation done at closing may not match your actual liability
  for either year, and the shortfall lands on you.
</p>

<h2>What to do before a deal, not during one</h2>

<ul>
  <li>Read your grant agreement for the change-of-control provision. Full acceleration, partial, double-trigger, or nothing — the answer changes the analysis entirely.</li>
  <li>Know your exercise price, grant date, and vested count for every tranche. Not approximately.</li>
  <li>If you exercised and held in a prior year, find out whether you have an unused AMT credit. People routinely forget these.</li>
  <li>Model an early exercise while the outcome is uncertain. Exercising before a deal starts the capital gain holding period, but it puts real money at risk on a company that may not sell, and it can create AMT in the year of exercise on a gain you have not received.</li>
  <li>If your Employer Equity is a meaningful part of your net worth, get advice at rumour stage. Once a letter of intent exists, the assignment-of-income doctrine and the deal timeline close most doors.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The short version</h2>

<p>
  In a cash acquisition, expect ordinary income and plan for the tax bill. In a stock deal,
  confirm in writing whether your options were assumed as ISOs or converted. In both, check
  the $100,000 limit against your acceleration clause, and watch the ninety-day window if you
  are leaving.
</p>
<p>
  None of this requires a deal to be underway. All of it is harder once one is.
</p>

</Article>
